COURSES = {}

COURSES['sustainable-performance'] = dict(
    slides=[
        ('Performance depends on recovery',
         ['Effort without recovery does not last', 'Energy is the real resource', 'Sustainable beats heroic'],
         "Welcome to Sustainable Performance. Many people believe that doing well means working longer. In practice, output depends on energy as much as on hours. Effort without recovery works for a short sprint, but it wears people down over months. This lecture is about doing good work in a way you can keep up. We will cover how to plan around energy, how to set boundaries you can keep, how to notice overload early, and how to build small recovery habits into an ordinary week."),
        ('Energy is the real resource',
         ['Time is fixed, energy is not', 'Notice when you do your best thinking', 'Protect that time for important work'],
         "You cannot add hours to the day, but you can change how much energy you bring to them. Most people have a pattern. Some think best early, others in the afternoon. Spend a week noticing when your focus is sharpest and when it dips. Then protect your best hours for work that needs real thought, and use the low-energy times for routine tasks like admin and email. Planning your week around energy, not just time, often gets more done in fewer hours, and leaves you less drained at the end of the day."),
        ('Four kinds of energy',
         ['Physical: sleep, movement, food', 'Emotional: how you feel', 'Mental: focus and attention', 'Purpose: why the work matters'],
         "It helps to think of energy in four kinds. Physical energy comes from sleep, movement and food, and it underpins everything else. Emotional energy is how you feel, which is affected by relationships and by stress. Mental energy is focus, and it is drained by constant switching between tasks. Purpose energy comes from knowing why your work matters. When you feel flat, ask which of the four is low. The answer points to a different fix each time. A short walk helps physical energy, while a clearer goal helps purpose."),
        ('Boundaries you can keep',
         ['A boundary is a clear, kind limit', 'Offer an alternative where you can', 'Start with one you can actually hold'],
         "A boundary is a clear, kind limit on what you will do and when. It is not a refusal to help. A healthy boundary often sounds like, I can do that on Thursday, or, I can do the first half today. Offering an alternative keeps the relationship positive. The common mistake is setting a boundary so ambitious that you break it within days, which teaches you to ignore your own limits. Start with one that you can actually hold, such as not checking messages after a set time, and build from there."),
        ('Recognising overload early',
         ['Trouble switching off', 'Irritability and cynicism', 'Small tasks feel heavy'],
         "Overload builds slowly, so the early signs are easy to miss. Watch for trouble switching off in the evening, a shorter temper, a growing cynicism about work, or small tasks that suddenly feel heavy. Changes in sleep and appetite are also common signals. The key is to notice the trend, not a single bad day. If you see several signs for more than a couple of weeks, treat it as information and act. Talk to someone, reduce a commitment, or ask for support. Waiting until you are exhausted makes recovery much longer."),
        ('Small recovery habits',
         ['Short breaks between demanding tasks', 'A real end to the working day', 'One thing each week that restores you'],
         "Recovery does not have to mean a holiday. Short breaks between demanding tasks let your attention reset. A clear end to the working day, such as closing the laptop and taking a short walk, helps your mind switch off. And one thing each week that genuinely restores you, whether that is exercise, time with friends, or a hobby, gives you something to look forward to. These habits are small, but they add up. The key is to schedule them like meetings, because if they are optional, they are the first thing to go."),
        ('Talking about workload',
         ['Be specific about what is on your plate', 'Ask what can wait', 'Raise it early, with options'],
         "If your workload is too high, saying so early is a strength, not a weakness. Be specific. Instead of saying you are overwhelmed, list what is on your plate and what each item needs. Ask your manager or colleague what can wait or be dropped. Bring options rather than only a problem, such as moving a deadline or sharing a task. Most managers prefer to hear about pressure when there is still room to adjust. Keeping quiet until something breaks is harder for everyone, including you."),
        ('Your next steps',
         ['Track your energy for one week', 'Choose one boundary to hold', 'Complete the workbook and the final quiz'],
         "To finish, track your energy levels for one week, noting when you feel sharp and when you feel flat. Choose one boundary that you will hold, and tell someone about it. Complete the workbook for this course, which includes an energy log and a weekly planning page. Then take the final quiz. When you reach the end of the last lesson you will receive your certificate and a personal review of how you worked through the course. Sustainable performance is built from small, steady habits, so be patient and keep going."),
    ],
    glossary=[
        ('Energy', 'The capacity you bring to work: physical, emotional, mental and purpose-related.'),
        ('Boundary', 'A clear, kind limit on what you will do and when.'),
        ('Overload', 'A sustained state where demands exceed your capacity to recover.'),
        ('Recovery', 'Activities that restore energy, from short breaks to rest days.'),
    ],
    reflect=[
        'At what time of day do you do your best thinking, and how much of that time is protected?',
        'Which boundary have you set and broken, and what would a more realistic version look like?',
        'What are your first two signs of overload, and who could you tell?',
    ],
    exercise=('One-week energy log', [
        'Three times a day, rate your energy from one to five.',
        'Note what you were doing and what you had eaten or slept.',
        'At the end of the week, mark your two best hours of the day.',
        'Move one important task into those hours next week.',
    ]),
)

COURSES['manager-wellbeing-conversations'] = dict(
    slides=[
        ('Why managers have well-being conversations',
         ['You see changes early', 'People often will not raise it first', 'Your role is to listen and signpost'],
         "Welcome to Well-being Conversations for Managers. Managers are often the first to notice when someone is struggling. A change in mood, in quality of work, or in attendance can all be signals. Many people will not raise a problem themselves, because they worry how it will look. A short, kind conversation can make a real difference. This lecture covers how to open the conversation, how to listen without trying to fix, how to know your limits, and how to point people to the right support."),
        ('Your role and its limits',
         ['You are not a therapist', 'You are not expected to diagnose', 'You can listen, support and signpost'],
         "It is important to be clear about your role. You are not a therapist or a doctor, and you are not expected to diagnose anything. What you can do is notice, ask, listen and point towards help. Knowing this takes pressure off. You do not need to have the answers. You need to be willing to have the conversation, to take it seriously, and to follow up. That mix of care and clear limits protects both the person and you, and it keeps the conversation useful rather than awkward."),
        ('Opening the conversation',
         ['Choose a private, unhurried moment', 'Start with what you have noticed', 'Ask an open question'],
         "Choose a private, unhurried moment, not the end of a busy meeting. Start with what you have noticed, in neutral terms, for example, I have noticed you have seemed quieter lately. Then ask an open question, such as, how are things, really. Avoid questions that push for an answer, like, what is wrong with you. Make it clear that there is no pressure to share. Some people will talk straight away, and others will not. Saying that the door is open, and following up later, is often what makes the next conversation easier."),
        ('Listening without fixing',
         ['Let them finish', 'Reflect back what you heard', 'Resist offering solutions straight away'],
         "The instinct of many managers is to jump to solutions. In a well-being conversation, that can feel dismissive. Instead, let the person finish. Reflect back what you heard, for example, it sounds like the workload has been weighing on you. Ask whether they would like advice, or just to be heard. Silence is fine. When people feel listened to, they often work out their own next step. If a practical change is needed, such as adjusting a deadline, you can agree it together once they have said what they need."),
        ('Useful phrases and ones to avoid',
         ['Helpful: that sounds hard, thank you for telling me', 'Avoid: I know exactly how you feel', 'Avoid: just try to stay positive'],
         "Language matters. Helpful phrases include, that sounds really hard, thank you for telling me, and, what would help right now. Phrases to avoid include, I know exactly how you feel, because you probably do not. Also avoid, just try to stay positive, and, other people have it worse. These shut the conversation down. Keep your tone steady and unhurried. You do not have to say the perfect thing. A genuine, simple response, such as, I am glad you told me, is usually enough to show you care."),
        ('Knowing your limits',
         ['Do not promise confidentiality you cannot keep', 'Know when to involve others', 'Take immediate risk seriously'],
         "Be honest about what you can promise. You should not promise absolute confidentiality, because if there is a risk to someone's safety, you may need to involve others. Say what you can keep private and when you would need to share. Know your organisation's process and who to contact, such as HR or an employee assistance line. If someone is in immediate danger or mentions harming themselves, treat it as urgent and follow your emergency procedures. Knowing your limits is part of caring for the person well."),
        ('Signposting and following up',
         ['Know the support available', 'Offer, do not push', 'Check in again within a week'],
         "Signposting means pointing someone to the right support. Learn what your organisation offers, such as counselling, an employee assistance programme, or occupational health, and also what external services exist. Offer the information and let the person choose. Then follow up. A short check in within a week, how are you doing, shows that the first conversation was not a one-off. Keep a brief private note of what you agreed. Following up is often the most valued part, because it shows that you meant what you said."),
        ('Your next steps',
         ['Find your organisation\'s support options', 'Prepare an opening you are comfortable with', 'Complete the workbook and the final quiz'],
         "To finish, find out what support your organisation offers, and write down the contact details so you have them ready. Prepare an opening sentence for a well-being conversation that feels natural in your own words. Complete the workbook for this course, which includes a conversation guide and a follow up planner. Then take the final quiz. When you reach the end of the last lesson you will receive your certificate and a personal review. These conversations get easier with practice, and your willingness to start them matters."),
    ],
    glossary=[
        ('Signposting', 'Pointing someone to appropriate support without taking on the role of counsellor.'),
        ('Open question', 'A question that invites more than a yes or no answer, such as "how are things, really?"'),
        ('Reflective listening', 'Repeating back what you heard in your own words to show you understood.'),
        ('Employee assistance programme', 'A confidential support service some employers provide for staff.'),
    ],
    reflect=[
        'What stops you from asking someone how they are, really?',
        'When someone shares a problem, do you tend to fix it or listen, and what is the effect?',
        'Who in your organisation could you contact if someone needed more support than you can give?',
    ],
    exercise=('Conversation plan', [
        'Write an opening sentence that mentions what you noticed, in neutral terms.',
        'Write two open questions you could ask.',
        'List the support options you could signpost to.',
        'Plan when you will check in again.',
    ]),
)

COURSES['ei-in-practice'] = dict(
    slides=[
        ('Emotions in everyday decisions',
         ['Emotion is data, not noise', 'Unnoticed feelings steer decisions', 'Name it, then decide'],
         "Welcome to Emotional Intelligence in Practice. This course assumes you know the basics, and focuses on using them in real situations: decisions, feedback and conflict. We start with the idea that emotions are data, not noise. A feeling of unease before a meeting may be pointing at something real. A feeling of excitement may be clouding your judgement. Unnoticed feelings steer decisions quietly. When you notice and name them, you can take them into account without letting them take over."),
        ('Reading your emotional signals',
         ['Notice the body first', 'Ask what the feeling is about', 'Decide whether it helps this decision'],
         "Emotions often show up in the body before the mind. A tight chest, a clenched jaw, a knot in the stomach. Learn your own early signals. When you feel one, ask what it is about. Is it the situation, the person, or something older that the situation reminds you of. Then ask whether the feeling helps with the decision in front of you. Sometimes it does, for instance caution before a risky move. Sometimes it does not, such as irritation about something unrelated. Sorting the two is a skill that improves with use."),
        ('Staying steady in a heated conversation',
         ['Slow your breathing and your pace', 'Name the emotion in the room', 'Return to the shared goal'],
         "When a conversation heats up, the first job is to steady yourself. Slow your breathing and slow your speech, because your pace sets the pace for everyone. If the emotion is obvious, name it calmly, for example, I can see this matters a lot to both of us. Then return to the shared goal, which is usually a good result for the team or the customer. Avoid arguing about who is right. The aim is not to win the point, it is to reach a solution you can both live with, while keeping the relationship intact."),
        ('Feedback without the sting',
         ['Be specific about the behaviour', 'Describe the impact, not the character', 'Invite their view'],
         "Feedback often triggers defensiveness because it feels like a judgement of the person. To reduce that, describe the specific behaviour, not a trait. Say, in the last two meetings you interrupted before others finished, not, you are rude. Describe the impact, for example, the team has stopped sharing ideas. Then invite their view, since they may have context you lack. Defensiveness drops when people feel heard. If you are receiving feedback, listen fully first, thank the person, and take time before responding if you need it."),
        ('Working through conflict',
         ['Make both sides feel heard', 'Separate interests from positions', 'Agree a next step'],
         "Conflict is normal in any team that cares about its work. The first goal is to make both sides feel heard, which often reduces the heat by itself. Next, separate positions from interests. A position is what someone says they want. An interest is why they want it. Two people may disagree on the position but share the interest, such as a smooth launch. Once interests are on the table, options appear. Finish by agreeing a concrete next step, and a time to check whether it worked."),
        ('Influencing without authority',
         ['Understand what the other person cares about', 'Make a clear, specific request', 'Build trust before you need it'],
         "Much of working life involves influencing people who do not report to you. Start by understanding what they care about, and connect your request to it. Make the request clear and specific, including what you need, by when, and why. And build trust before you need it, by being reliable and helping others when you can. People are far more willing to support someone they trust. Avoid pressure tactics, which may work once, but damage the relationship. Respect and clarity are slower, but they last."),
        ('Putting it into practice',
         ['Choose one conversation you have been avoiding', 'Prepare the opening and the goal', 'Review how it went afterwards'],
         "The way to build these skills is to use them on real conversations. Choose one you have been avoiding. Prepare how you will open, what the shared goal is, and what you will do if the other person becomes upset. Have the conversation, then review. What went well, what would you change, what did you learn about the other person. Writing a few lines straight afterwards makes the learning stick. Each conversation makes the next one easier, and over a few months the change in how you handle difficult moments becomes clear."),
        ('Your next steps',
         ['Pick one conversation to have this week', 'Use the feedback structure', 'Complete the workbook and the final quiz'],
         "To finish, pick one conversation you will have this week and write down your opening. Use the feedback structure from this lecture. Complete the workbook for this course, which has a conflict planning sheet and a feedback template. Then take the final quiz. When you reach the end of the last lesson, you will receive your certificate and a personal review of how you worked through the course. Thank you for your time, and good luck with the conversations ahead."),
    ],
    glossary=[
        ('Position', 'What someone says they want in a disagreement.'),
        ('Interest', 'The reason behind a position, which often overlaps with what the other side wants.'),
        ('Defensiveness', 'A protective reaction to perceived criticism that makes listening harder.'),
        ('Influence', 'Moving a decision or action without relying on formal authority.'),
    ],
    reflect=[
        'Which emotion tends to influence your decisions without you noticing?',
        'What is the conversation you have been putting off, and what is the shared goal in it?',
        'How do you usually react when you receive feedback, and what would you like to change?',
    ],
    exercise=('Prepare a difficult conversation', [
        'Write the shared goal in one sentence.',
        'Write your opening using behaviour, impact and an invitation.',
        'List the other person\'s likely interests.',
        'After the conversation, note what went well and what you would change.',
    ]),
)

COURSES['emotionally-intelligent-leader'] = dict(
    slides=[
        ('Leaders set the weather',
         ['Your mood spreads through the team', 'People read you closely', 'Climate affects performance'],
         "Welcome to The Emotionally Intelligent Leader. A leader's mood tends to spread through a team. People watch how you react to news, how you handle pressure, and whether you listen. Researchers describe this as the leader setting the weather. A tense leader creates a tense team, and a steady leader makes it easier for others to think clearly. This lecture covers how to read and shape the climate of your team deliberately, how to stay calm under organisational pressure, and how to coach other managers to grow their own emotional intelligence."),
        ('Reading the climate of a team',
         ['Listen to what is not being said', 'Watch energy across the week', 'Ask people directly, and often'],
         "Before you can shape the climate, you need to read it. Listen to what is not being said, such as the topics people avoid. Watch the energy across the week. When does it rise, and when does it fall. Ask people directly, in one to ones and in small groups, how things feel. Short pulse questions help, as long as you act on the answers. Keep in mind that people tell leaders less than they tell each other. So the picture you see is usually more positive than the reality. Make it easy and safe to tell you the truth."),
        ('Shaping the climate on purpose',
         ['Decide the tone you want', 'Show it in small behaviours', 'Repeat it consistently'],
         "Once you can read the climate, decide what you want it to be, such as open, focused and calm. Then show it in small behaviours. How you start meetings, how you respond to bad news, how you thank people. Small, repeated actions shape culture more than speeches. Consistency matters, because people notice when words and behaviour differ. If you say you value candour, but react badly when someone disagrees, they will believe your reaction. Aim to be predictable in the ways that matter, so people feel safe around you."),
        ('Leading under pressure',
         ['Pressure travels downward', 'Pause before you pass it on', 'Say what you know and what you do not'],
         "Pressure on a leader travels downward. If you pass on urgency without thinking, it becomes anxiety for others. Before you communicate a difficult message, pause. Ask what the team needs to know, and what is still uncertain. Then be honest about both. Saying, here is what I know, here is what I do not know yet, and here is when I will update you, creates steadiness. People cope better with uncertainty than with silence. Look after your own recovery too, because a depleted leader has less to give."),
        ('Making it safe to speak up',
         ['Thank people who bring bad news', 'Respond to ideas with curiosity', 'Admit your own mistakes'],
         "Teams perform better when people can speak up. You create that safety through your reactions. Thank people who bring bad news, even if you do not like it. Respond to ideas with curiosity, by asking questions before judging. And admit your own mistakes openly, which shows that errors can be discussed. When a leader says, I got that wrong, here is what I learned, it gives permission to others. Safety is built slowly through many small responses, and it can be damaged quickly by one careless reaction, so stay aware."),
        ('Growing emotional intelligence in your managers',
         ['Coach in one to ones, not memos', 'Ask questions that build awareness', 'Give feedback on how they lead'],
         "If you lead managers, your impact multiplies through them. Emotional intelligence grows best through coaching, not through a memo or an annual test. Use your one to ones with managers to ask questions that build awareness. How do you think the team experienced that meeting. What did you notice in yourself. Give specific feedback on how they lead, including the effect on their team. Model the behaviour yourself, since managers copy what leaders do. Encourage them to practise in small moments, and to reflect on them regularly."),
        ('Your own development as a leader',
         ['Ask for honest feedback', 'Notice your patterns under stress', 'Build a reflection habit'],
         "Leaders need development too, and often get less feedback as they become more senior. Ask for honest feedback from people you trust, and make it clear that you want the truth. Notice your own patterns under stress, such as becoming controlling or withdrawn. A short reflection habit helps. At the end of each week, note one moment you handled well, and one you would handle differently. Over time, this builds the self-awareness that everything else depends on. The best leaders stay curious about their own impact."),
        ('Your next steps',
         ['Choose one climate behaviour to model', 'Plan one coaching question per manager', 'Complete the workbook and the final quiz'],
         "To finish, choose one behaviour that you will model consistently this month, such as how you open meetings. Write one coaching question for each manager who reports to you. Complete the workbook for this course, which includes a climate check and a reflection log. Then take the final quiz. When you reach the end of the last lesson, you will receive your certificate and a personal review of how you worked through the course. Thank you for listening, and good luck leading with awareness."),
    ],
    glossary=[
        ('Team climate', 'The shared feeling in a team that shapes how safe and energised people are at work.'),
        ('Pulse question', 'A short, frequent question used to read how the team is feeling.'),
        ('Coaching', 'Helping someone build their own awareness and skill through questions and feedback.'),
        ('Modelling', 'Showing through your own behaviour the standard you expect from others.'),
    ],
    reflect=[
        'How would your team describe the weather you create on a bad day?',
        'What is one behaviour of yours that people copy, for better or worse?',
        'Which manager would benefit most from a coaching conversation this month?',
    ],
    exercise=('Climate check', [
        'Ask three people to describe the mood of the team in three words.',
        'Compare their answers with how you would describe it.',
        'Choose one behaviour that could shift the climate in the direction you want.',
        'Repeat the check in a month.',
    ]),
)
